## Stage 4 — Habsiguda-Nacharam Corridor (Original Model)

### Environment Snapshot

```mermaid
graph LR
    J0["J0<br/>Habsiguda<br/>(T-junction)"] --- J2["J2<br/>Nagendra Nagar<br/>(4-way)"]
    J0 --- J1["J1<br/>Habsiguda Colony<br/>(4-way)"]
    J2 --- J3["J3<br/>ECIL X Roads<br/>(4-way)"]
    J3 --- J4["J4<br/>Nacharam X Roads<br/>(4-way)"]
    J4 --- J5["J5<br/>Mallapur<br/>(T-junction)"]

    style J0 fill:#1abc9c,stroke:#16a085,color:#fff
    style J1 fill:#3498db,stroke:#2980b9,color:#fff
    style J2 fill:#3498db,stroke:#2980b9,color:#fff
    style J3 fill:#3498db,stroke:#2980b9,color:#fff
    style J4 fill:#3498db,stroke:#2980b9,color:#fff
    style J5 fill:#1abc9c,stroke:#16a085,color:#fff
```

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| **Network** | [habsiguda_network.net.xml](file:///e:/github_projects/trafficRL/traffic_rl/sumo/habsiguda_network.net.xml) |
| **Location** | Habsiguda–Nacharam, Hyderabad, India |
| **Junctions** | 6 (2 T-junctions + 4 four-way) |
| **Arterial Length** | 4.8 km, 10 road segments |
| **Traffic** | 2000 vehicles/hr (realistic peak) |
| **Transfer Learning** | Weights from [multihead_dqn_best.pth](file:///e:/github_projects/trafficRL/models/multihead_dqn_best.pth) (200 ep pre-trained) |
| **Fine-Tuning** | LR: 0.00005, ε: 0.3→0.05, 100 episodes |
| **Env Class** | [HabsigudaSUMOEnv](file:///e:/github_projects/trafficRL/traffic_rl/env/habsiguda_env.py#23-455) (mixed junction types) |

### Results — Stage 4 (Original Pre-Trained Model, No Fine-Tuning)

| Metric | RL Agent | Fixed-Time Baseline | Change |
|--------|---------|--------------------:|--------|
| **Throughput (veh/h)** | 633.3 | 945.5 | -33.0% |
| **Vehicles Completed** | 633.6 | 946.0 | -33.0% |
| **Avg Waiting Time (s)** | 1.57 | 3.11 | **+49.5% ↓** |
| **Avg Queue Length** | 50.03 | 77.03 | **+35.1% ↓** |
| **Phase Changes** | 705.2 | 450.0 | +56.7% |

> [!WARNING]
> The original pre-trained model (transferred directly from single-intersection training) showed significant throughput reduction (-33%) on the corridor, though waiting times and queue lengths still improved dramatically vs fixed-time. This gap motivated fine-tuning.

